var CONTENT_VERSION = "1.0.0";
var KEY = {"b1":{"d":"brain","t":"Adolescent brain: prefrontal cortex","y":"hotspot","p":1,"m":"m1","s":"b1","h":["13d059bf510b05aa3bfb"],"x":"The prefrontal cortex handles planning, judgment and impulse control, and it is one of the last regions to finish maturing (into the mid-20s). Reward circuits are already very responsive in adolescence."},"b2":{"d":"brain","t":"Neurotransmission","y":"seq","p":1,"m":"m1","s":"b2","h":["0ad9f04c32235c3ab5fc"],"x":"Signals move as electricity inside a neuron, then as chemicals (neurotransmitters) across the synapse, then bind to receptors, and are cleared so the signal can end."},"b-def":{"d":"brain","t":"Tolerance, dependence and withdrawal","y":"match","p":1,"m":"m1","s":"b-def","h":["aa026b097d08973cf4d0"],"x":"Tolerance means needing more for the same effect. Physical dependence means the body has adapted. Withdrawal is the set of symptoms after stopping. The fourth description is the pattern of a substance use disorder (addiction), which is different from the other three."},"b3":{"d":"brain","t":"Dopamine, reward and reinforcement","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m1","s":"syn","h":["da0854bf91eefaf99e47"],"x":"More dopamine for longer means a stronger “this mattered, do it again” signal. That is reinforcement: the brain’s learning system marks the experience as worth repeating."},"b4":{"d":"brain","t":"Adolescent brain development","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m1","s":"b4","h":["bfb09986ded4883e9766"],"x":"Adolescent brains are still developing. That makes this a period of greater vulnerability, not a period of inability. Planning ahead and support help."},"c-tol":{"d":"brain","t":"Applying tolerance / dependence / withdrawal","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-tol","h":["7448aa922af5bbf66c36"],"x":"Needing more to get the same effect is tolerance. Nothing here shows stopping symptoms (withdrawal), loss of control, or harm (a substance use disorder)."},"c-dep":{"d":"brain","t":"Applying tolerance / dependence / withdrawal","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-dep","h":["1f69dbfdeb0d607ec675"],"x":"The body has adapted to the medicine (physical dependence), which is why a gradual taper is planned. Taking it as directed, with no cravings, loss of control or harm, is not an addiction pattern."},"c-wd":{"d":"brain","t":"Applying tolerance / dependence / withdrawal","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-wd","h":["7070923f205cfc77e88f"],"x":"Symptoms that appear when a regularly used substance is stopped or reduced are withdrawal. They show the body has adapted (dependence), but on their own they do not prove a substance use disorder."},"c-rein":{"d":"brain","t":"Reinforcement","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-rein","h":["f53baebf9aab6cf95530"],"x":"A rewarding experience followed by thinking about and seeking it again is reinforcement. One use and one day of wanting it is not enough to show dependence or a substance use disorder."},"c-sud":{"d":"brain","t":"Addiction / substance use disorder","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-sud","h":["78d948385ec25d5ddbe6"],"x":"Several features appear together over time: loss of control, failed attempts to cut back, time spent, giving up activities, and use despite harm. That is the kind of pattern clinicians look for. Treatment works, and a professional makes the formal assessment."},"c-ins1":{"d":"brain","t":"Not enough evidence","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-ins1","h":["9839abdf00c0f11fb449"],"x":"Frequent use for two weeks is worth noticing, but it does not show control problems, harm, tolerance, or withdrawal. A friend’s label is an opinion. The responsible conclusion is that there is not enough evidence."},"c-ins2":{"d":"brain","t":"Not enough evidence","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m1","s":"c-ins2","h":["27ab288956bd8b9b5747"],"x":"Using something every day, or calling oneself “addicted” as a joke, is not evidence of a disorder. We would need information about control, harm, and what happens when the person stops."},"b6":{"d":"brain","t":"Risk and protective factors","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m1","s":"b6","h":["c0e76b39f4985cfa14bc"],"x":"Risk factors raise the chance of problems and protective factors lower it, but neither is destiny. Some things, like changing schools, can be stressful or can open new support, depending on the situation."},"b7":{"d":"brain","t":"Addiction as a treatable health condition","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m1","s":"b7","h":["87c9a8a3f217872e2a93"],"x":"Addiction involves changes in brain circuits and many risk and protective factors, not just willpower. It is treatable, and relapse is a common part of a chronic condition, not proof of failure."},"n1":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Nicotine and tobacco products","y":"match","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"n1","h":["6ee19c7bce2059de36b4"],"x":"Cigarettes and cigars burn tobacco. Smokeless tobacco and pouches are used in the mouth and are not burned. E-cigarettes heat a liquid into an aerosol. All of these can deliver addictive nicotine. Gum and patches are medications to help adults quit."},"n2":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Smoke vs. aerosol vs. “water vapor”","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m2","s":"n2","h":["3a6d509fdd43a0da12e6"],"x":"Burning tobacco creates tar and carbon monoxide. Heating e-liquid makes an aerosol of tiny particles, which can include nicotine, flavoring chemicals and metals. It is not just water vapor."},"n3":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Relative risk","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m2","s":"n3","h":["8bc62fe21e6566da82f6"],"x":"Products differ in risk, and that is useful for adults who already smoke. It does not make any product safe or appropriate for youth, because nicotine harms adolescent brain development and can cause addiction."},"n4":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Nicotine and the adolescent brain","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"n4","h":["db794e2c8cef33cb6a76"],"x":"Nicotine activates reward pathways and can interfere with brain areas for attention, learning, mood and impulse control while they are still developing. Adolescent exposure is linked to addiction and to higher risk of later drug use."},"n5":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Changing tobacco use, attitudes and policy","y":"seq","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"n5","h":["af2967315b6153cf840a"],"x":"The order is 1964 (Surgeon General’s report), 1971 (broadcast ad ban), 2009 (FDA authority), 2015 (pod devices spread), 2019 (minimum age raised to 21)."},"g1p":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Historical decline in cigarette smoking","y":"predict","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"g-adult","h":["e1714e8e9329da745b2e"],"x":"The line fell from about 19% (2010) to 15% (2015), a drop of about 1 percentage point per year, which points to roughly 10 to 14% by 2022. The actual 2022 value was 11.6%."},"g1b":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Interpreting tobacco trend data; limits of graphs","y":"multi","p":2,"m":"m2","s":"g-adult","h":["4d68fc68b4fee07ee169"],"x":"The graph shows adult cigarette smoking only. It cannot show what caused the change, what teens did, or whether people switched to other products. It can show that 2022 is lower than 1965 and how steady the decline was."},"g2a":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Rise and decline of youth vaping","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m2","s":"g-youth","h":["59176e04cb254f6be282"],"x":"Both lines show real changes, but a graph of two trends cannot show that one caused the other. A decline from a peak does not mean use is zero or that nicotine is no longer a concern."},"g2b":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Interpreting statistics","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"g-youth","h":["c0700b9a81c3f0e0e100"],"x":"The drop is 19.7 percentage points. As a share of the starting value, 19.7 ÷ 27.5 is about 0.72, so about a 70 percent decrease."},"a1":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Alcohol as a depressant","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a1","h":["541627fe920236d50f30"],"x":"Alcohol slows the central nervous system. The early “excitement” comes from lowered inhibition and judgment. Reaction time, coordination, memory and (at high amounts) breathing are slowed."},"a2":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Standard drinks","y":"match","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a2","h":["12dd364acd51942bf5c9"],"x":"Standard drinks depend on both size and strength: 12 oz × 5% = 1; 5 oz × 12% = 1; 16 oz × 5% ≈ 1⅓; 24 oz × 8% ≈ 3."},"a-r2":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Reaction time and impairment","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m3","s":"sim-react","h":["7b9dd15a488836d832d3"],"x":"Twenty feet is more than a car length, and reaction time is only one part of impaired driving (judgment, vision and coordination are affected too). A simplified model can show a pattern, but it cannot say who is safe to drive. No amount of drinking is “safe” for driving."},"a-b":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Pace, food, body size and BAC","y":"multi","p":3,"m":"m3","s":"sim-bac","h":["4c8376b9b28b1360832a"],"x":"The model shows that pace and food change the peak but not how long clearing takes, and that body size matters. It has no coffee setting because coffee does not change BAC. A model also cannot show that any number is safe to drive: impairment begins well below 0.08, and real BAC varies widely."},"a-tab":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Impairment at different BAC levels","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-tab","h":["4fc3f01d73b43791ff10"],"x":"Even small amounts affect judgment and attention. Laws set limits for enforcement (and drivers under 21 face zero-tolerance rules), but the body does not have a switch at 0.08."},"a-myth":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Myths about sobering up","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-myth","h":["ef29a68754581f5427d2"],"x":"Coffee, showers and exercise can make a person feel more awake but do not remove alcohol. Only time lowers BAC. Whether a specific number of drinks passes a limit depends on body size, time, food and other factors."},"a-long":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Longer-term effects; alcohol use disorder","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a-long","h":["71107acb6bf3e94e00a5"],"x":"Heavy, long-term drinking is linked to liver disease, heart problems, several cancers and alcohol use disorder, a treatable medical condition."},"as-drive":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Impaired driving","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"as-drive","h":["3914dca62299e2d2370f"],"x":"Feeling fine is not a reliable test, and judgment is one of the first things alcohol affects. Walking tests, water and slow driving do not remove the risk. A different ride does."},"as-consent":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Consent and capacity","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"as-consent","h":["d1d49978ccec8ea518e6"],"x":"Consent requires the ability to understand and decide. Heavy impairment removes that capacity, and consent can be withdrawn at any time."},"as-poison":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Alcohol poisoning and emergency response","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"as-poison","h":["83fe264c1d395da5c8b2"],"x":"Being hard to wake, slow or irregular breathing, vomiting and pale or bluish skin are signs of alcohol poisoning, which can stop breathing. This is an emergency: call 911, stay, and keep the airway clear."},"as-aggr":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Conflict and aggression","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"as-aggr","h":["7e309d709261e9dd7c06"],"x":"Alcohol impairs the brain areas that regulate impulses and emotions. De-escalating, creating distance and involving an adult lowers the risk of injury."},"ae-black":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Blackouts and memory","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"ae-black","h":["d8742a21cd28228ea1be"],"x":"In a blackout, a person can act awake but the brain is not recording new memories. It usually follows heavy, rapid drinking and signals serious impairment."},"ae-meds":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Medications and alcohol","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"ae-meds","h":["ced391dd2c31572b57b3"],"x":"Alcohol interacts with many prescription and over-the-counter medications and can intensify sedation or other risks. A pharmacist or clinician can say what applies to a specific medication."},"ae-binge":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Binge drinking","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"ae-binge","h":["e918c33dd7aec2054217"],"x":"Binge drinking is defined by the pattern and the BAC it produces, not by the type of drink. It raises risk of injury, alcohol poisoning and blackouts."},"ae-factors":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Factors affecting impairment","y":"multi","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"ae-factors","h":["f54488d625a819273c4e"],"x":"Pace, body size, food, medications and individual differences all matter, as does the actual amount. Feeling confident is not a factor and can be misleading because alcohol affects judgment."},"c1":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Cannabis: variation in effects","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m4","s":"c1","h":["d2a647e408932b7c3632"],"x":"Dose, potency, route, frequency and individual factors all matter. “Natural” describes a source, not safety, and feeling confident does not predict how someone will be affected."},"cs-drive":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Cannabis and driving","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m4","s":"cs-drive","h":["6e9a7c5304b5272cc5e4"],"x":"Cannabis affects attention, reaction time, coordination and judgment, and people are poor judges of their own impairment. Being experienced may change how impaired someone feels, not how well they drive."},"cs-edible":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Cannabis: route and timing","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m4","s":"cs-edible-scene","h":["75cc01b090660533c689"],"x":"Eaten cannabis can take from 30 minutes to a couple of hours to produce effects, which can then last for hours. Taking more because “nothing happened” is a common way people end up with an unexpectedly strong reaction."},"cs-teen":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Cannabis, the adolescent brain and cannabis use disorder","y":"mc","p":3,"m":"m4","s":"cs-teen","h":["f7e23ca876cb2d72ec79"],"x":"Cannabis can affect attention, memory and learning, and the adolescent brain is still developing. Starting young and using often raise the risk of cannabis use disorder, but only a professional can say whether a disorder is present."},"r1":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Prescription medications: use vs. misuse","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"r1","h":["73c6787db3f5f0de9e6c"],"x":"Appropriate use means the right person, the prescribed dose, and the intended purpose. Using someone else’s medication, taking more than prescribed, or using it for a different reason is misuse."},"r2":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Stimulant misuse and academic performance","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"r2","h":["b9a31d6adbfc9d471e33"],"x":"Prescription stimulants can strain the heart and sleep, and taking them without a prescription removes medical oversight. They are not a reliable shortcut to better academic performance."},"od1":{"d":"cannabisRx","t":"Other drug concepts; polysubstance use","y":"match","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"od1","h":["2a6420cb36f040f87d05"],"x":"Stimulants strain the heart. Hallucinogens alter perception, thinking and mood unpredictably. Inhalants can seriously harm the heart, brain and breathing even when the products are common. Opioids plus alcohol compound breathing depression. Substances do not cancel each other out; a stimulant can mask warning signs while the risks add up."},"o1":{"d":"opioid","t":"Respiratory depression","y":"hotspot","p":1,"m":"m5","s":"o1","h":["e0154079885e7878867c"],"x":"The brainstem controls automatic functions such as breathing. Opioids can slow breathing (respiratory depression) until oxygen levels drop dangerously."},"o3":{"d":"opioid","t":"Fentanyl and counterfeit pills","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"o3","h":["1d87ad6c3ffa5127b0c0"],"x":"Fake pills are made in illegal settings with no quality control, so one pill can have little fentanyl and its twin can have a deadly amount (about 2 mg can be lethal for some people). Color and imprint can be copied. Only a pharmacy-dispensed prescription is reliable."},"o4":{"d":"opioid","t":"Naloxone","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"o4","h":["eaa41971441c2b5d53cb"],"x":"Naloxone works only on opioids and its effect is temporary (often 30 to 90 minutes), so emergency care is still needed. It has no meaningful effect if no opioid is present, and it does not treat addiction."},"ov1":{"d":"opioid","t":"Overdose warning signs","y":"multi","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"sim-od","h":["7346602650c512403301"],"x":"Unresponsiveness, slow or stopped breathing, gurgling or snoring sounds, and pale, blue or gray skin are classic warning signs. The phone and the spilled drink are irrelevant, and fast talking and sweating point away from opioid overdose."},"ov2":{"d":"opioid","t":"Immediate help-seeking","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m5","s":"sim-od","h":["c17bf25bd5a854326a0b"],"x":"Every minute without enough oxygen matters. Call 911 immediately. Details about what was taken can be shared with the dispatcher, but they should never delay the call."},"ov3":{"d":"opioid","t":"Emergency response sequence","y":"pick","p":3,"m":"m5","s":"sim-od","h":["5eba19cf2f02bfee676e"],"x":"Check for a response, call 911, give naloxone if available, support breathing and positioning, and stay until help arrives. Coffee, cold showers, walking and waiting do not reverse an overdose, and waiting costs minutes the person may not have."},"p1":{"d":"decision","t":"Direct and indirect pressure; perceived norms","y":"sort","p":1,"m":"m6","s":"p1","h":["4f9ea9a2c241ee5a3c68"],"x":"Direct pressure is a person asking, urging or taunting you. Indirect pressure comes from the environment: media, what you think others do (perceived norms, which are often higher than the real numbers), and fear of rejection."},"p2":{"d":"nicotine","t":"Marketing, flavors, product design, social media","y":"multi","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"p2","h":["8fee843171bb05619415"],"x":"Flavors, discreet device design and social belonging messages all increase appeal to young people. The ad contains no health evidence and no prominent warning. Noticing these techniques is the first step in resisting them."},"rf-vape":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: direct offer","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-vape","h":["b8ad025e9fdbd7263a6c"],"x":"A short, calm no, a repeat or an alternative if they push, and a move to another place or person are realistic and effective. “Maybe later” leaves the door open, and insults or long lectures raise the pressure."},"rf-pill":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: unidentified pill","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-pill","h":["d0a065c549900b1f3bc9"],"x":"You cannot know what is in an unidentified pill. A short no, a steady repeat, and moving to people and places that feel safe protect you. Bargaining (“half”) and “maybe” keep the offer alive."},"rf-drink":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: group offer","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-drink","h":["6ad5a44c27f23594212e"],"x":"A brief, friendly no, repeated if needed, with a plan to leave using a safe ride keeps you safe without a fight. “Maybe a sip” invites more pressure."},"rf-ride":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: unsafe ride","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-ride","h":["5e3b59e684f4d24a206c"],"x":"Seatbelts and slow driving do not fix impaired judgment and reaction time. Declining the ride and arranging another one does. Offering help to the driver shows care without enabling the drive."},"rf-chat":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: group-chat pressure","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-chat","h":["a6315efe029921d1753c"],"x":"You do not owe a group chat an essay. A short no, an alternative plan, and a good plan for your evening work. “I’ll see how I feel” invites more pressure, and going “just to leave later” is a weaker plan than choosing a different evening."},"rf-study":{"d":"decision","t":"Refusal skills: peer offers a prescription","y":"slots","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"rf-study","h":["c788baa55174d0dc63c6"],"x":"Your friend’s medicine is for your friend. A short no, an alternative way to help each other study, and real support for stress are safer than taking it."},"p-chat":{"d":"decision","t":"Pressure simulation","y":"run","p":5,"m":"m6","s":"sim-chat","h":["20da119cee8fc59eaa4a","b3f295e179276037067b","4bb5434f1d38db33f68c","f72ac5ff18f868065ef5","f76d7928ff2bb72ccca2","96997bd66d7c017b8527","01b64d9a0a4676d6eb3a","00462a3bff31d94a494e","534be53fcb55c38ab725","2e26ddaf5def7303ee12","f0a54f644e4209908c21","32e38f351c73e148ce6b","b4bc2f3437c4085df586","be00335ba3d93923406f","db753684cb07cd00f6a0","9a041abf80f6905e7260","64c84167075543a0d9d3","f436859410277025de08","090cd8168ef5159ce15b","103d79a77eca69087e9c","9b1d9c986a4096d10430","1aef59076c3cc8bd8c7b","94afad85f88303766dac","61732ae2f28327e766f6","c94b0acdf9bdcc0b9a99","bfd9702b948f0ad23763","16b40ae6197030f81b83","2d68a3a1ec7af463788d"],"x":"Strong runs use short, assertive refusals, offer an alternative or an exit, and reach for support (a parent or other trusted adult) before getting in an unsafe ride. Giving in, riding with a driver who has been drinking, or being left without a safe way home all reduce safety."},"p-help":{"d":"decision","t":"Helping without enabling; when to get help","y":"multi","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"p-help","h":["eaa4447b13f5bcb31047"],"x":"Unresponsiveness, slow breathing, repeated vomiting with inability to stay awake, and impaired driving are safety emergencies or imminent dangers. Getting help is caring, not betraying. A boring party or mild jitters from caffeine is not an emergency."},"cl1c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: vaping aerosol","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-vapor-s","h":["e574f66a84abb281d0f9"],"x":"The claim says “harmless.” The source says the aerosol can contain nicotine, fine particles, flavoring chemicals and metals. The claim is contradicted by the evidence."},"cl1f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Rewriting misleading claims","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-vapor-s","h":["cfe21f53295be1fde9b4"],"x":"An accurate rewrite fixes the exaggeration without swinging to another one. Aerosol contains more than water, and the products are not identical to cigarettes in every way."},"cl2c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: “natural”","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-nat-s","h":["90c93c4c4e89d34c97f8"],"x":"Being natural does not make something safe (many natural substances are poisonous). The NIDA excerpt describes effects on attention, memory and learning and a risk of cannabis use disorder, so the claim is not supported."},"cl2f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Rewriting misleading claims","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-nat-s","h":["c109b4905b7168a77a1e"],"x":"A good rewrite is specific and accurate, without scare language. Effects vary by dose, potency, age and other factors."},"cl3c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: naloxone","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-narcan-s","h":["3db861dc1809536d837f"],"x":"The excerpt says naloxone is temporary and that emergency care is still needed, so “fine afterward, no hospital, a cure” is not supported."},"cl3f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Rewriting misleading claims","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-narcan-s","h":["8ce867cc16fb5c8ffa24"],"x":"Naloxone is a valuable, temporary tool. It is not a substitute for emergency care."},"cl4c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: correlation vs. causation","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-corr-s","h":["e5c79d14a394c87c9e4d"],"x":"The study found an association (vapers had lower average GPA). It was a one-time survey that did not measure other factors, so it cannot show that vaping caused the grades to drop. The headline overstates it."},"cl4f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Correlation vs. causation","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-corr-s","h":["25d5387d0ef26b6a7560"],"x":"Association is not causation. Other factors might drive both, and a single survey cannot show the order of events. Sample size and capital letters are details, not the main problems."},"cl5c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: trend vs. “solved”","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-solved-s","h":["229e11a874ffe66580b0"],"x":"The decline in the graph is real, so part of the post is supported. But 7.8% is still a substantial number of students, and a graph of past use cannot show that prevention efforts are no longer needed. “Solved” is an exaggeration."},"cl5f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Interpreting statistics","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-solved-s","h":["e87324aba2fd37283404"],"x":"The graph shows a decline. It does not show that the problem is solved, that prevention is unneeded, or why use fell."},"cl6c":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: counterfeit pills","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-fake-s","h":["cc2fcaf26be940f7f87c"],"x":"The claim is modest, matches the DEA excerpt, and does not overstate. It is supported."},"cl6f":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evidence vs. exaggeration","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"cl-fake-s","h":["c84582b98e622bbfdda8"],"x":"The DEA found that amounts vary widely. “Every pill” would exaggerate, and color cannot reliably tell a fake pill apart from a real one."},"el1":{"d":"literacy","t":"Source credibility","y":"seq","p":1,"m":"m7","s":"sim-evidence","h":["577d410da15ff526e3e7"],"x":"A primary report with methods and data is the most reliable. A news article summarizing it is next. A seller has a financial interest. An anonymous comment gives no way to check anything."},"el2":{"d":"literacy","t":"Evaluating claims: missing context and exaggeration","y":"mc","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"sim-evidence","h":["62ea505bfa46d582d84d"],"x":"Youth e-cigarette use did fall, so part of the claim is supported. But about 1.6 million students still reported use in 2024, other nicotine products exist, and the Surgeon General’s evidence on brain harm has not changed. “Not a problem anymore” exaggerates a real decline and leaves out important context."},"f1":{"d":"opioid","t":"Synthesis: triage and immediate danger","y":"seq","p":2,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["d9a8db6dc5d46c2ef57f"],"x":"An unresponsive person with slow breathing is a life-threatening emergency right now. An impaired driver about to leave is the next most imminent danger. An offered pill is a risk you can decline immediately. Someone else vaping nearby is the least urgent for you."},"f2":{"d":"decision","t":"Synthesis: direct and indirect influences","y":"sort","p":2,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["5c763766dc193558cde2"],"x":"An offer made to you is direct pressure. Seeing others vape and seeing a post that makes it seem normal are indirect influences (modeling and perceived norms). The playlist and the dog have nothing to do with the decisions."},"f3":{"d":"opioid","t":"Synthesis: counterfeit pill","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["885216291190e7ea9513"],"x":"Imprints and stories can be faked, and counterfeit pills are not made with quality control. Only a pharmacy-dispensed prescription for you can be trusted."},"f4":{"d":"alcohol","t":"Synthesis: transportation","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["86c813a4c5898568b4d7"],"x":"A sober driver or a different ride removes the risk. Water, coffee and time do not make an impaired driver safe, and seatbelts do not prevent crashes."},"f5":{"d":"literacy","t":"Synthesis: evaluating a claim","y":"mc","p":1,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["890b31611f3532e5b875"],"x":"The post gives no evidence, comes from an unknown account, and conflicts with the facts in front of you."},"f6":{"d":"opioid","t":"Synthesis: emergency response","y":"pick","p":2,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["d4de8ec7a4d116e5809a"],"x":"Call 911 first, give naloxone if available, then support breathing and position until help arrives. Details about what was taken can be shared with the dispatcher."},"f7":{"d":"decision","t":"Synthesis: consequence mapping","y":"match","p":2,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["1d624cea9e79eca5906d"],"x":"Each decision changes the risk. Unknown pills can contain unpredictable fentanyl. Impaired driving raises crash risk. Calling 911 gets help. Leaving someone unresponsive can be fatal."},"f8":{"d":"decision","t":"Synthesis: protective factors and supports","y":"multi","p":1,"m":"m8","s":"gathering","h":["bfe4ce5f95924b8391e0"],"x":"A friend who is not using, an adult you can call, and a practiced refusal are protective. Unsupervised settings and larger crowds are not protective."}};
